# Getting started

Install Pontia, connect your device to Pontia Cloud, and start using your coding agents from the Remote Dashboard.

## Requirements

The release installer supports **x86_64 and ARM64 Linux** and **Intel and Apple Silicon macOS 11 or later**. Linux lifecycle commands require a running systemd user service manager; macOS uses a per-user LaunchAgent.

The installer requires `curl`, `grep`, `install`, `jq`, `mktemp`, `mv`, `openssl`, `rm`, `tar`, `tr`, `uname`, and `wc`, plus `sha256sum` on Linux or `shasum` on macOS. OpenSSL must support Ed25519 verification; on macOS the installer automatically detects Homebrew's `openssl@3` in its standard Intel and Apple Silicon locations.

Install the clients you plan to use before initialization:

- **pi:** install the `pi` CLI and `tmux`.
- **Codex:** install the `codex` CLI. Automatic Codex startup uses systemd on Linux and a per-user LaunchAgent on macOS.

## Install Pontia

Run the signed release installer:

```sh
curl -fsSL https://get.pontia.dev/install.sh | sh
```

The installer places `pontia` and `pontiad` in `$HOME/.local/bin` by default. Make sure that directory is included in your `PATH`.

## Initialize your device

Run the interactive initializer:

```sh
pontia init
```

The initializer lets you:

1. Select the pi integration, Codex integration, or both.
2. Generate the local dashboard credential.
3. Start the Pontia background service.
4. Sign in to Pontia Cloud and register your device for the Remote Dashboard.

On first initialization, Pontia uses your home directory as the Workspace Browser root. Existing roots are preserved when you run the initializer again.

When prompted to sign in and enable remote access, accept and complete the browser authorization. Select an available edge and confirm your device name. Pontia opens the Remote Dashboard when setup completes.

For an agent or script, follow [Initialization](/docs/initialization) to install Client integrations, start the service, and register remote access with separate commands instead of answering wizard prompts.

## Check the service

```sh
pontia status
```

Use `pontia down` to stop the service and `pontia up` to start it again.

## Update Pontia

Update `pontia` and `pontiad` to the latest signed stable release on Linux or macOS:

```sh
pontia update
```

If the managed service is running, Pontia restarts it and waits for it to become healthy. If replacement or restart fails, Pontia restores the previous binaries and attempts to recover the service.

Open your device's Remote Dashboard and follow the [Dashboard guide](/docs/dashboard) to activate a Workspace and start your first Session. If device registration is not yet complete, finish the [remote access setup](/docs/remote-access) first.
